    shataka: "Śṛṅgāraśataka",
    deva: "भोगे रोगभयं कुले च्युतिभयं वित्ते नृपालाद्भयं\nमाने दैन्यभयं बले रिपुभयं रूपे जराया भयम् ।\nशास्त्रे वादिभयं गुणे खलभयं काये कृतान्ताद्भयं\nसर्वं वस्तु भयान्वितं भुवि नृणां वैराग्यमेवाभयम् ॥",
    translit: "Bhoge rogabhayaṃ kule cyutibhayaṃ vitte nṛpālādbhayaṃ\nMāne dainyabhayaṃ bale ripubhayaṃ rūpe jarāyā bhayam |\nŚāstre vādibhayaṃ guṇe khalabhayaṃ kāye kṛtāntādbhayaṃ\nSarvaṃ vastu bhayānvitaṃ bhuvi nṛṇāṃ vairāgyamevābhayam ||",
    meaningEn: "In pleasure there is fear of disease; in noble birth, fear of fall; in wealth, fear of the king; in honour, fear of humiliation; in strength, fear of enemies; in beauty, fear of old age; in learning, fear of rivals; in virtue, fear of the wicked; in the body, fear of death. Everything in the world is attended by fear — only renunciation is fearless.",
    explanationEn: "This verse bridges the Śṛṅgāraśataka and Vairāgyaśataka in spirit. Bhartuhari lists nine prized possessions and pairs each with its specific terror. The architecture is perfect: nine fears, then one release. What makes the verse startling is that it includes the good things — beauty, learning, virtue — alongside pleasure and wealth. Even the best things are hostages to fortune. The only state beyond fear is vairāgya, not because it is an escape, but because it has no stake in what can be taken away.",
    meaningMr: "भोगात रोगाची भीती; कुळात पतनाची भीती; धनात राजाची भीती; मानात अपमानाची भीती; बळात शत्रूची भीती; रूपात वृद्धत्वाची भीती; शास्त्रज्ञानात वादकाची भीती; गुणांत दुर्जनाची भीती; देहात मृत्यूची भीती. जगात सगळ्या गोष्टी भीतीने भरलेल्या — फक्त वैराग्यच निर्भय आहे.",
