    translit: "Kṣaṇaṃ bālā bhūtvā kṣaṇamapi yuvatī bhūya taruṇī\nKṣaṇaṃ pakvaṃ keśaṃ śithilatanutā cintitamanā |\nPunarbālaḥ pumān iti paramavicintya manasā\nNarāṇāṃ jīvitaṃ vāyuvivikta ivābhram ||",
    meaningEn: "For a moment a child, for a moment a young girl, then a young woman; for a moment greying hair, loosening body, anxious mind. And then, reflecting deeply, one sees that a man too follows this same course. Human life is like a cloud dispersed by the wind.",
    explanationEn: "A meditation on impermanence from within the Śṛṅgāraśataka — the book of desire — which is why it stings. Bhartuhari watches the beloved's beauty as something already in motion through time. She will be young, then middle-aged, then old; he will be the same. The cloud image is precise: a cloud dispersed by wind doesn't vanish dramatically — it simply thins, becomes less defined, until there is only sky. Life goes that way, with no clear moment of ending.",
    meaningMr: "एक क्षण बालपण, एक क्षण तरुणपण, एक क्षण युवती; एक क्षण पांढरे केस, सुटणारी काया, चिंताग्रस्त मन. आणि मग विचार केला की पुरुषाचंही तेच. माणसाचं जीवन म्हणजे वाऱ्याने विखुरणारं ढग.",
