    translit: "Dhanyāste ye bhartur āśāṃ na kurvanti vilāsinyo\nYe vā bhartur āśāṃ na gaṇayanti priyaṃvadāḥ |\nĀsāṃ madhye kā nu śreyaskarī paritāpinī\nBhartā no bhoktā iti manyate yā svayaṃ sā ||",
    meaningEn: "Blessed are those women who do not depend on their husband's expectations, and those who, sweet-spoken though they are, do not count on their husband's desires. Among these, who is the more praiseworthy? She who thinks from within herself: \"My husband is not my enjoyer.\"",
    explanationEn: "A remarkable verse of proto-feminist consciousness from within the Śṛṅgāraśataka. Bhartuhari praises the woman who thinks for herself — who does not define herself through her husband's desires or expectations. The woman who inwardly asserts \"my husband is not my master-enjoyer\" is praised as śreyaskarī — the one who has achieved the better state. This sits interestingly within a book otherwise full of male-gaze imagery, suggesting that Bhartuhari's vision of love included the recognition of female interiority and autonomy.",
