    translit: "Kaṣṭaṃ kaṇṭhamanupraviśya hariṇīnetre tavedam vacaḥ\nMuñcatyaśru ca hanta candramukhi me cetaścirāyākulam |\nTat kiṃ syāt karuṇārdrayā tvamabale yadyātmanaḥ prāpyase\nUtkaṇṭhāvaśagena kiṃ karaṇīyaṃ jīvena bāṇārditam ||",
    meaningEn: "With difficulty I speak these words, reaching your deer-like eyes — and ah, tears flow, O moon-faced one, and my long-troubled heart is undone. What would it be if you, moved by compassion, could be reached by me? What can this life do, pierced by longing's arrows, at the mercy of yearning?",
    explanationEn: "A verse of helpless petition. The lover speaks with great difficulty — the words barely emerge through the constriction of emotion. The deer-like eyes receive what the mouth can barely say, and tears follow before speech. Then the final devastating question: what is this life worth, stabbed through by longing, when it cannot reach you? The directness of the address — speaking to the beloved within the verse — gives it an immediacy unusual even in Sanskrit śṛṅgāra.",
    meaningMr: "कठीणपणे घशातून शब्द काढून तुझ्या हरिणीसारख्या डोळ्यांपर्यंत पोहोचवतो — अरे, चंद्रमुखी, अश्रू येतात आणि माझं दीर्घकालीन व्याकुळ मन मोडतं. तू जर करुणेने ओलावलेली माझी होशील — तर काय व्हायचं ते होऊ दे. पण बाणांनी जखमी आणि उत्कंठेच्या आहारी गेलेल्या या जीवाने काय करायचं?",
