    translit: "Kvacid bhūmau śayyā kvacidapi ca paryaṅkaśayanaṃ\nKvacit śākāhāraḥ kvacidapi ca śālyodanaruciḥ |\nKvacit kanthādhārī kvacidapi ca divyāmbaradharo\nMansvī kāryārthī na gaṇayati duḥkhaṃ na ca sukham ||",
    meaningEn: "Sometimes sleeping on the ground, sometimes on a fine bed; sometimes eating only greens, sometimes enjoying fine rice; sometimes wearing patched rags, sometimes fine divine garments — the person of noble purpose, intent on their goal, counts neither sorrow nor joy.",
