    deva: "संसारे स्वप्नतुल्ये सति परिणामे पुत्रमित्रकलत्रे ।\nजालायमाने मायामये च विषये प्रबुद्धो भव ।\nक्षीयन्ते सर्वसम्पत् वयसि गतवति व्याधिभिश्चाभिभूत\nतस्माज्जाग्रत सावधाना भवत मतिमन्तो नरोत्तमाः ॥",
    translit: "Saṃsāre svapnatulye sati pariṇāme putramitrakalatre |\nJālāyamāne māyāmaye ca viṣaye prabuddho bhava |\nKṣīyante sarvasampat vayasi gatavati vyādhibhiścābhibhūta\nTasmājjāgrata sāvadhānā bhavata matimanto narottamāḥ ||",
    meaningEn: "This world is like a dream in its eventual nature; sons, friends, and wife are like nets; objects are illusions. Be awakened. All wealth declines when old age comes and disease overwhelms — therefore, be alert, be careful, O wise and excellent persons.",
    explanationEn: "One of the Vairāgyaśataka's most direct and urgent verses. Bhartuhari drops all metaphorical indirection: the world is a dream, relationships are nets, sense objects are illusions. And then the factual: wealth declines with age, disease comes. The verse does not say life is worthless — it says it is temporary and that its temporary nature is the most important thing to understand. The call \"jāgrata\" (be awake) is Bhartuhari's final and recurring instruction.",
    meaningMr: "हा संसार स्वप्नासारखा आहे; पुत्र, मित्र, पत्नी जाळ्यांसारखे आहेत; विषय मायिक आहेत — जागे व्हा. सगळी संपत्ती म्हातारपण आणि आजारपणाने क्षीण होते — म्हणून सावध व्हा, सतर्क व्हा, हे बुद्धिमान श्रेष्ठ माणसांनो.",
